% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part I: Foundations
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{Foundations}

\noindent Every compiler is an implementation of a language standard, and the
standard is a legal document: ambiguous, self-referential, and full of
subtle constraints that only become visible when you try to encode them
as executable rules.  This part pins down the normative corpus that
\texttt{kcc} implements --- the final draft of ISO/IEC~9899:2018 (C17)
--- and the conformance model that governs how a compiler may interpret
the standard's requirements, undefined behaviour, and
implementation-defined choices.  No code appears here; the chapters that
follow lay the contractual ground on which every subsequent line of
assembly rests.

\begin{multicols}{2}
@i literate/foundations/01-normative-corpus.weft
\end{multicols}
\newpage

\begin{multicols}{2}
@i literate/foundations/02-conformance-model.weft
\end{multicols}


% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part II: Machine
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{The Machine}

A compiler that targets bare metal must build its own floor.  This part
constructs the runtime substrate on which the rest of \texttt{kcc}
stands: the ARM64 calling conventions and macro vocabulary
(Chapter~\ref{ch:arm64-conventions}), thin wrappers around the Darwin
\texttt{svc~\#0x80} system-call interface (Chapter~\ref{ch:syscalls}),
a bump-pointer arena allocator backed by \texttt{mmap}
(Chapter~\ref{ch:arena}), the three fundamental data structures every
compiler needs --- hash table, dynamic vector, and string intern table
(Chapter~\ref{ch:data-structures}) --- plus byte-level string
operations (Chapter~\ref{ch:strings}), buffered I/O
(Chapter~\ref{ch:io}), a diagnostic engine that formats
\texttt{file:line:col: severity: message} errors to stderr
(Chapter~\ref{ch:diagnostics}), and the source file manager that
\texttt{mmap}s C source files for zero-copy lexing
(Chapter~\ref{ch:source}).  None of these modules depend on any C
library; every byte of output reaches the kernel through \texttt{svc}.

\begin{multicols}{2}
