% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part III: The Frontend
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{The Frontend}

The frontend transforms a stream of bytes into a structured
representation of the program.  It follows the C17 translation phases
almost to the letter: trigraph replacement and line splicing
(Chapters~\ref{ch:source-encoding}--\ref{ch:line-splicing}), tokenisation into the
roughly one hundred token types defined by the grammar
(Chapter~\ref{ch:lexer}), preprocessing with macro expansion and
conditional compilation (Chapter~\ref{ch:preprocessor}), string
literal processing and adjacent-literal concatenation
(Chapters~\ref{ch:string-literals}--\ref{ch:concatenation}), and finally recursive-descent
parsing of declarations, expressions, and statements into an abstract
syntax tree (Chapters~\ref{ch:parser}--\ref{ch:statements}).  The
parser is hand-written --- no generator tools --- so every production
rule is visible in the assembly, and error recovery follows a simple
synchronisation-point strategy.

