% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part IV: Semantics
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{Semantics}

Parsing proves that a program is syntactically well-formed; semantic
analysis proves that it is \emph{meaningful}.  This part implements the
type system (Chapter~\ref{ch:types}), with singleton descriptors for
every built-in type and constructors for pointers, arrays, functions,
structs, unions, and enums; the symbol table
(Chapter~\ref{ch:symtab}), with separate scoped name spaces for
ordinary identifiers and tags as required by \stdref{6.2.3}; the
semantic analyser (Chapter~\ref{ch:sema}), which walks the AST
checking declarations, statements, and expressions against the
language's type rules; and the implicit conversion module
(Chapter~\ref{ch:conversions}), which implements the standard's array,
function, and lvalue decay rules.  After this pass, every AST
expression node carries its resolved type, and any constraint
violation has been reported as a diagnostic.

