% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part V: Intermediate Representation
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{Intermediate Representation}

Between the tree-shaped AST and the flat machine code lies the
intermediate representation: a control-flow graph of basic blocks,
each containing a linear sequence of three-address instructions in
static single-assignment (SSA) form.  Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-design}
defines the IR vocabulary --- constants, arithmetic, comparisons,
memory operations, branches, calls, and allocas --- together with the
data structures for functions, basic blocks, and instructions.
Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-builder} walks the typed AST and emits the
corresponding IR, lowering C control flow into explicit branches and
phi-less SSA.  Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-validation} provides a structural
integrity check that verifies every basic block is properly terminated
before the IR is handed to later phases.

\begin{multicols}{2}
@i literate/ir/31-ir-design.weft
\end{multicols}
