% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part VI: Optimization
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{Optimization}

The IR produced by the builder is correct but na\"ive: it loads and
stores every temporary through a stack slot, and it faithfully
translates constant sub-expressions that could be folded at compile
time.  This part applies two classical optimisation passes over the IR
before code generation.  The pass manager (Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-passes})
iterates over all functions and drives the individual passes.
Constant folding and peephole simplification
(Chapter~\ref{ch:peephole}) evaluate arithmetic on constants at
compile time and replace redundant instruction patterns.  Dead code
elimination (Chapter~\ref{ch:dce}) uses a mark-and-sweep algorithm
over the use--def chains to remove instructions whose results are
never consumed.  Together these passes eliminate the most obvious
waste without introducing the complexity of a full register allocator
or loop optimiser.

\begin{multicols}{2}
@i literate/optimization/34-ir-passes.weft
\end{multicols}
