% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
% Part VII: The Backend
% ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
\part{The Backend}

The backend closes the loop: it lowers the optimised IR into ARM64
machine code and packages the result as a Mach-O object file that
Apple's linker can consume.  The code generator
(Chapter~\ref{ch:codegen}) uses a simple all-spill strategy --- every
virtual register lives in a stack slot, and three scratch registers
(\texttt{x9}, \texttt{x10}, \texttt{x11}) shuttle values between
slots and ALU --- trading runtime performance for implementation
simplicity.  The ARM64 encoder (Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-arm64}) provides
functions that pack each instruction class into its 32-bit binary
encoding.  The Mach-O writer (Chapter~\ref{ch:emit-macho}) assembles the
object file: header, load commands, code section, relocations, symbol
table, and string table.  The linker-info module
(Chapter~\ref{ch:linker-info}) manages relocations and symbols during code
generation, and the compilation driver
(Chapter~\ref{ch:compile-driver}) orchestrates the full pipeline from
source file to \texttt{.o}.

